{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgMgPfW64vwE36Rda4eo6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WHXfLtZb6bvGXthDeCwsFF","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-grader-y6cvqbnk/report.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":223,"cache_read_input_tokens":6171,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":223},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4788f095-223a-4668-be43-f00d686fd3dd","uuid":"0721f3b5-8505-408e-a95f-3db24034aaab","timestamp":"2026-10-24T20:18:20.934Z","request_id":"req_011CgMgPfExyAqRmgRC9GP8w","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01WHXfLtZb6bvGXthDeCwsFF":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-grader-y6cvqbnk/report.md"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WHXfLtZb6bvGXthDeCwsFF","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Review of `repo/service/routes.mjs`\n2\t\n3\tThe component is small, and I found three real issues. The first is the most serious.\n4\t\n5\t## 1. Unhandled promise rejection can crash the process (High, availability)\n6\t`routes.mjs:16-25`: the `GET /mcp` and `DELETE /mcp` handlers are `async` and `await session.handleRequest(req, res)` with no `try/catch`.\n7\t- Express 4.22.2 doesn't forward rejected promises from async handlers to `next`. The Express docs in `research/` say this.\n8\t- The app installs no `unhandledRejection` hook. Node 20's default mode (`throw`) turns the rejection into an uncaught exception, which terminates the process.\n9\t- Any error thrown or rejected by `handleRequest` therefore kills the server for every session. A malformed request, a transport that has already closed, or a client that disconnects mid-stream could each trigger it. Only a holder of the gateway secret can reach these handlers, but a single trusted client can still take the service down.\n10\t- Fix: wrap each handler in `try/catch`, calling `next(err)` or returning a 500 if headers haven't been sent. Alternatively, use a shared `wrap(fn)` helper.\n11\t\n12\t## 2. Sessions are never cleaned up (Medium, resource exhaustion)\n13\t- `POST /mcp/init` (`:10-14`) adds a transport to `sessions` on every call and never removes it.\n14\t- `DELETE /mcp` delegates to the transport but doesn't remove the map entry. Sessions also have no TTL and no cap.\n15\t- Even for the single trusted principal, a buggy or looping client leaks memory and any resources held by `createTransport()`. A closed transport also stays reachable, which makes issue 1 more likely.\n16\t- Fix: delete the entry after a successful `DELETE` and on transport close. Add an idle timeout and a maximum session count.\n17\t\n18\t## 3. Session lookup uses a plain object (Low–Medium, robustness)\n19\t- `const sessions = {}` is looked up with a client-controlled header (`:17`, `:22`).\n20\t- A header like `__proto__`, `constructor` or `toString` resolves to an inherited property, which is truthy. `session.handleRequest` is then not a function, and the resulting `TypeError` becomes the unhandled rejection from issue 1. That makes it a crash trigger that needs no valid session.\n21\t- The header can also be an array or `undefined`.\n22\t- Fix: use `new Map()` or `Object.create(null)`, and check that the header is a string.\n23\t\n24\t## Minor observations\n25\t- **Secret comparison (`:7`):** `!==` on the Authorization header isn't constant-time. This is low risk for a high-entropy secret behind a gateway, but `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length buffers is cheap to add.\n26\t- **No response on a failed init:** `createTransport()` at `:12` can throw synchronously. Express 4 does catch synchronous errors in non-async handlers, so this is fine as written, but the session ID shouldn't be registered until creation succeeds. It currently is registered after creation, so that ordering is correct.\n27\t- **Session ID is not bound to anything:** this is acceptable per the README (one trusted principal, with IDs as correlation handles only). I didn't count it as a finding.\n28\t- **No `POST /mcp` handler:** only `/mcp/init`, `GET` and `DELETE` are registered, so the transport can't receive client-to-server JSON-RPC messages over `POST`. If the host's `createTransport` expects that, it's a functional gap. I can't confirm it from this repo.\n29\t\n30\t## Checked and not an issue\n31\t- **Auth gating:** `app.use('/mcp', …)` covers `/mcp/init`, `GET /mcp` and `DELETE /mcp`, and it runs before dispatch. A missing `GATEWAY_KEY` fails closed at startup.\n32\t- **`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` redirect advisory (GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw):** not applicable. The package isn't a dependency, and this component is a server, not an SDK HTTP client.\n33\t- **Manifest `sensitive` option:** there's no manifest in this repo, so it doesn't apply.\n34\t- **Logging:** the code writes no logs, so no credentials leak there.\n35\t- **Out of scope per the README:** the `createTransport` implementation, the gateway and secret store, host audit, framing limits, ingress policy and process supervision. These need host evidence.\n36\t\n37\tI read the files only and ran nothing."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4788f095-223a-4668-be43-f00d686fd3dd","uuid":"e3b5078e-a65e-422c-8e4f-3d14f19ad9e6","timestamp":"2026-10-24T20:18:20.954Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-mcp-grader-y6cvqbnk/report.md","content":"# Review of `repo/service/routes.mjs`\n\nThe component is small, and I found three real issues. The first is the most serious.\n\n## 1. Unhandled promise rejection can crash the process (High, availability)\n`routes.mjs:16-25`: the `GET /mcp` and `DELETE /mcp` handlers are `async` and `await session.handleRequest(req, res)` with no `try/catch`.\n- Express 4.22.2 doesn't forward rejected promises from async handlers to `next`. The Express docs in `research/` say this.\n- The app installs no `unhandledRejection` hook. Node 20's default mode (`throw`) turns the rejection into an uncaught exception, which terminates the process.\n- Any error thrown or rejected by `handleRequest` therefore kills the server for every session. A malformed request, a transport that has already closed, or a client that disconnects mid-stream could each trigger it. Only a holder of the gateway secret can reach these handlers, but a single trusted client can still take the service down.\n- Fix: wrap each handler in `try/catch`, calling `next(err)` or returning a 500 if headers haven't been sent. Alternatively, use a shared `wrap(fn)` helper.\n\n## 2. Sessions are never cleaned up (Medium, resource exhaustion)\n- `POST /mcp/init` (`:10-14`) adds a transport to `sessions` on every call and never removes it.\n- `DELETE /mcp` delegates to the transport but doesn't remove the map entry. Sessions also have no TTL and no cap.\n- Even for the single trusted principal, a buggy or looping client leaks memory and any resources held by `createTransport()`. A closed transport also stays reachable, which makes issue 1 more likely.\n- Fix: delete the entry after a successful `DELETE` and on transport close. Add an idle timeout and a maximum session count.\n\n## 3. Session lookup uses a plain object (Low–Medium, robustness)\n- `const sessions = {}` is looked up with a client-controlled header (`:17`, `:22`).\n- A header like `__proto__`, `constructor` or `toString` resolves to an inherited property, which is truthy. `session.handleRequest` is then not a function, and the resulting `TypeError` becomes the unhandled rejection from issue 1. That makes it a crash trigger that needs no valid session.\n- The header can also be an array or `undefined`.\n- Fix: use `new Map()` or `Object.create(null)`, and check that the header is a string.\n\n## Minor observations\n- **Secret comparison (`:7`):** `!==` on the Authorization header isn't constant-time. This is low risk for a high-entropy secret behind a gateway, but `crypto.timingSafeEqual` on equal-length buffers is cheap to add.\n- **No response on a failed init:** `createTransport()` at `:12` can throw synchronously. Express 4 does catch synchronous errors in non-async handlers, so this is fine as written, but the session ID shouldn't be registered until creation succeeds. It currently is registered after creation, so that ordering is correct.\n- **Session ID is not bound to anything:** this is acceptable per the README (one trusted principal, with IDs as correlation handles only). I didn't count it as a finding.\n- **No `POST /mcp` handler:** only `/mcp/init`, `GET` and `DELETE` are registered, so the transport can't receive client-to-server JSON-RPC messages over `POST`. If the host's `createTransport` expects that, it's a functional gap. I can't confirm it from this repo.\n\n## Checked and not an issue\n- **Auth gating:** `app.use('/mcp', …)` covers `/mcp/init`, `GET /mcp` and `DELETE /mcp`, and it runs before dispatch. A missing `GATEWAY_KEY` fails closed at startup.\n- **`@modelcontextprotocol/sdk` redirect advisory (GHSA-6prh-2h8m-c8cw):** not applicable. The package isn't a dependency, and this component is a server, not an SDK HTTP client.\n- **Manifest `sensitive` option:** there's no manifest in this repo, so it doesn't apply.\n- **Logging:** the code writes no logs, so no credentials leak there.\n- **Out of scope per the README:** the `createTransport` implementation, the gateway and secret store, host audit, framing limits, ingress policy and process supervision. These need host evidence.\n\nI read the files only and ran nothing.","numLines":37,"startLine":1,"totalLines":37}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WHXfLtZb6bvGXthDeCwsFF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
